n_blunders":59,"per100":11.2,"blunders_per100":1.83,"avg_pawn_loss":0.57,"games":98,"avg_opponent_rating":1852},{"month":"2026-05","n_moves":281,"n_mistakes":34,"n_blunders":10,"per100":12.1,"blunders_per100":3.56,"avg_pawn_loss":0.66,"games":8,"avg_opponent_rating":1782},{"month":"2026-06","n_moves":714,"n_mistakes":68,"n_blunders":11,"per100":9.52,"blunders_per100":1.54,"avg_pawn_loss":0.53,"games":18,"avg_opponent_rating":1760},{"month":"2026-08","n_moves":2065,"n_mistakes":190,"n_blunders":31,"per100":9.2,"blunders_per100":1.5,"avg_pawn_loss":0.51,"games":59,"avg_opponent_rating":1837}],"misc":{"wins":138,"losses":162,"draws":13,"time_forfeit_losses":38,"forfeit_pct":23.5}},"report":{"generated_at":1786752000,"based_on":{"games":301,"mistakes":1060},"verdicts":[{"kind":"weakness","headline":"The middlegame is your weakest phase","detail":"13.9 mistakes per 100 moves against 10.1 overall (580 mistakes across 4,160 moves, z = +8.2), and 0.66 pawns lost per move against 0.56 overall. Worst as White at 14.5 per 100. The damage concentrates on moves 11–20, immediately after preparation ends.","confidence":"high","training":{"label":"Drill middlegame mistakes","url":"train.html?phases=middlegame&judgments=blunder,mistake&focus=1","available":219}},{"kind":"weakness","headline":"Hanging pieces are your costliest recurring error","detail":"132 mistakes left a piece where it could simply be taken, at 3.5 pawns each — a minor piece per occurrence on average. This is the single largest identifiable leak, and it is a board-vision habit rather than a gap in understanding.","confidence":"high","training":{"label":"Drill hanging pieces","url":"train.html?motif=hanging_piece&judgments=blunder,mistake&focus=1","available":68}},{"kind":"weakness","headline":"You allow more forks than you miss","detail":"141 fork-related mistakes at roughly 3.1 pawns each. 96 were forks you allowed against 45 you missed, so the priority is checking what his knight and queen reach after your intended move, not spotting your own tactics faster.","confidence":"high","training":{"label":"Drill forks, both directions","url":"train.html?motif=missed_fork,allowed_fork&judgments=blunder,mistake&focus=1","available":67}},{"kind":"weakness","headline":"Endings with queens on are your most expensive positions","detail":"0.93 pawns lost per move — the highest figure anywhere on the board and nearly double your pure rook endings — with 11.6 mistakes per 100 and 45% scored across 94 games. Practical conclusion: when equal or better, trade queens.","confidence":"high","training":{"label":"Drill queens-on endgames","url":"train.html?endgame=queen+%2B+pieces&judgments=blunder,mistake&focus=1","available":42}},{"kind":"weakness","headline":"The Indian Defense is your worst result as White","detail":"35% across 30 games with 12.4 mistakes per 100 (z = +2.3), against 64.9% in the Queen's Pawn Game. The gap is repertoire rather than ability — there is no consistent system in place against ...Nf6 and ...g6.","confidence":"medium","training":{"label":"Drill Indian Defense games","url":"train.html?opening=Indian%20Defense&color=white&judgments=blunder,mistake&focus=1","available":44}},{"kind":"strength","headline":"Opening play is your strongest phase","detail":"0.24 pawns per move as Black and 0.33 as White, against 0.56 overall, with 4.75 mistakes per 100 as Black (z = −6.8 across 1,474 moves). The Caro-Kann accounts for 99 games and reliably delivers a sound position by move 12.","confidence":"high","training":{"label":"Drill your opening-phase mistakes","url":"train.html?phases=opening&judgments=blunder,mistake&focus=1","available":38}},{"kind":"strength","headline":"Simplified endings are where you play best","detail":"Pure pawn endings: 0.36 pawns per move and two mistakes in 161 moves. Pure rook endings: 6.8 mistakes per 100 (z = −2.3) and 59.5% scored over 37 games. Knight endings: 11 wins from 15. Accuracy rises as material comes off, which makes simplification a practical weapon.","confidence":"medium","training":{"label":"Drill rook endgames","url":"train.html?endgame=rook&judgments=blunder,mistake&focus=1","available":18}}],"narrative_md":"## How cost is measured\n\nEverything below is in **pawns lost per move**: the valuation after the best move minus the valuation after the move played, averaged over every move made. Overall figure: **0.56 pawns per move**. A 3-pawn move is a piece thrown away, 0.2 is a minor imprecision. Slips too small to be flagged as mistakes still count here.\n\n## Strengths to protect\n\n- **Opening play.** 0.24 pawns per move as Black, 0.33 as White, against 0.56 overall. The Caro-Kann structures are well understood across 99 games.\n- **Simplified endings.** Pure pawn endings 0.36 per move; pure rook endings 0.55 with 59.5% scored; knight endings 11 wins from 15. Accuracy improves as pieces come off.\n- **Recent form.** August is the cleanest month of the year — 0.50 pawns per move and 8.9 mistakes per 100, against opponents no weaker than before.\n\n## Repairs, in priority order\n\n**1. The middlegame, moves 11–20.** Cost per move doubles once theory ends: 0.28 pawns on moves 1–10 against 0.58 on moves 11–20, with the mistake rate going from 5.7 to 13.5 per 100. The opening is played from memory and the middlegame without a question to answer. At move 12, settle two things before choosing a move: where you break, and where he breaks. A modest plan beats drifting.\n\n**2. The blunder check.** 132 hanging pieces at 3.5 pawns each and 96 forks walked into at 3.2. This is discipline, not knowledge. Before releasing a move, scan the position it would create and ask three questions in order:\n\n- **Checks** — what checks does he have after my move?\n- **Captures** — what of mine is undefended or defended too few times?\n- **Forks** — where can his knights and queen land next, and what two things would they hit?\n\nOnly then play. Run it on every move, not only on sharp ones — most of these losses happened in quiet positions. Mate-related misses cost over 7 pawns each, so when either king is exposed, run it twice.\n\n**3. Trade queens when equal or better.** Queens-on endings cost 0.93 pawns per move and score 45% across 94 games; pure rook endings cost 0.55 and score 59.5%. Simplification is the higher-percentage practical choice.\n\n**4. The quiet endgame leak.** By mistake count the endgames look healthy, but they still shed 0.68 pawns per move — as much as the middlegame. Fewer catastrophes, steadier bleeding. In technical positions, calculate the short lines to the end instead of trusting feel.\n\n**5. The clock.** 37 of 157 losses are flag-falls, nearly one in four, and the time drains in the same moves 11–20 where the mistakes concentrate. Target reaching move 20 with 90 seconds.\n\n**6. A system against the Indian.** 35% across 30 games against 64.9% in the Queen's Pawn Game. Choose one setup against ...Nf6 and ...g6 and keep it. The 44 stored failures in those games repeat, so start there.\n\n## Weekly plan\n\n- **Daily:** 10 middlegame focus puzzles.\n- **Twice weekly:** one motif block, alternating hanging pieces and forks.\n- **Weekly:** the queens-on endgame set, plus 20 minutes replaying Indian Defense failures.\n- **Every game:** the blunder check above, and 90 seconds on the clock at move 20.\n\nTargets over the next 30 games: middlegame under 12 mistakes per 100, overall cost under 0.50 pawns per move."},"report_stale":false}